<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hygelac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 521</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Chlochilaicus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hygelac is the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> in the Old English poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>, the hero's uncle and lord. The poem describes his death on a raid into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, an event that matches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>' account of a Scandinavian king, Chlochilaicus, killed by the Frankish prince Theudebert in the early 6th century. He is therefore one of the few figures of Beowulf generally thought to be historical.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hygelac.jpg" alt="Hygelac" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Fouquet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_entre_Francs_et_Danois_en_515.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a> gives Hygelac's genealogy. According to the poem he was the son of Hrethel and had two brothers, Herebeald and Hæþcyn, as well as an unnamed sister who married Ecgtheow and was the mother of the hero Beowulf. Hygelac married Hygd, and they had a son, Heardred, and an unnamed daughter who married Eofor. His name appears in Old Norse as Hugleikr and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sources as Chlochilaicus or Hugilaicus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The poem tells of a war with the Swedes in which Hæþcyn was killed. Hygelac arrived at Hrefnesholt one day too late to save his brother, but he rescued the surviving Geatish warriors, who were besieged by the Swedish king Ongentheow and his three sons. The Swedes took refuge in a hill fort, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> stormed, and Eofor killed the Swedish king in the fighting. Hygelac ruled the Geats until he was killed during a raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, after which, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>, Heardred succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The raid allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/n-f-s-grundtvig">N. F. S. Grundtvig</a> to date Hygelac's death, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> records a raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdoms</a> by a king Chlochilaicus in the reign of Theodericus I, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>. The raiders were caught by a force under Theodericus's son Theodebertus, and the survivors fled to sea so hastily that they left their dead, their king among them. Gregory reports that the corpse, awe-inspiring for its height, was long exhibited at a Merovingian court. Gregory calls him a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, while the later Liber historiae Francorum calls him a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a> does.</p><p>The early eighth-century Liber Monstrorum also names him, as Higlacus, a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> whom no horse could carry from the age of twelve, and whose bones were shown to travellers on an island at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Leonard Neidorf argues that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a> and the Liber Monstrorum drew on a shared oral tradition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. Other scholars hold that the poem depends on the Liber historiae Francorum, and Walter Goffart estimated that it could not have been written with these details before 923.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hygelac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The raid of King <b>Hygelac</b> into Frisia is mentioned by Gregory of Tours and can be dated to around 521.</blockquote><a href="/work/beowulf">Beowulf · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy and Jordanes mention Gautoi and Gautigoths in southern Scandinavia, and the Old English poem Beowulf makes the Geats and their king <b>Hygelac</b> its heroes.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-geats">Kingdom of the Geats</a></li></ul></section>
